**1.1 — HIGH — `src/routes/api/public/hooks/resume-runs.ts:61-117, 258-290`**

This is the single largest DB-load contributor found. It's a `pg_cron every minute` endpoint. For every blocked/running mission (up to `MISSION_BATCH=50`, two separate passes), it calls `runStatusesOf(id)` and `pendingGatesOf(id)` — each a separate round trip — sequentially, one mission at a time. Worst case: ~200 sequential DB calls, every single minute, before any real work runs, and it scales with tenant count, not with `MISSION_BATCH`.

```ts
// before — up to 100 sequential round trips in this pass alone
for (const bm of blockedMissions) {
  const runStatuses = await runStatusesOf(bm.id);
  const pendingGateCount = await pendingGatesOf(bm.id);
  if (classifyMissionGate({ status: "blocked", runStatuses, pendingGateCount }) === "unblock") { /* ... */ }
}

// after — 2 batched queries total for the whole pass
const ids = blockedMissions.map((m) => m.id);
const { data: allRuns } = await admin.from("agent_runs").select("mission_id,status").in("mission_id", ids);
const runsByMission = new Map<string, string[]>();
for (const r of allRuns ?? []) runsByMission.set(r.mission_id, [...(runsByMission.get(r.mission_id) ?? []), r.status]);

const { data: allGates } = await admin.from("agent_approvals").select("mission_id").eq("status", "pending").in("mission_id", ids);
const pendingByMission = new Map<string, number>();
for (const g of allGates ?? []) pendingByMission.set(g.mission_id, (pendingByMission.get(g.mission_id) ?? 0) + 1);

for (const bm of blockedMissions) {
  const runStatuses = runsByMission.get(bm.id) ?? [];
  const pendingGateCount = pendingByMission.get(bm.id) ?? 0;
  if (classifyMissionGate({ status: "blocked", runStatuses, pendingGateCount }) === "unblock") { /* ... */ }
}
```
Apply the same shape to the block pass at `:258-290`. Turns ~200 queries/tick into ~4.

**1.2 — `src/lib/ai/eval-runner.server.ts:157-226`** — `runEvalSuite` inserts one `eval_case_results` row per test case (3 call sites: success/error/catch). Model calls must stay sequential (judge depends on subject output) but the write doesn't:
```ts
// after
const rows: Record<string, unknown>[] = [];
for (const c of cases) {
  // ...model calls unchanged...
  rows.push({ run_id: runId, case_id: c.id, user_id: userId, status, /* ... */ });
}
if (rows.length) await supabase.from("eval_case_results").insert(rows);
```

**1.3 — `src/lib/lineage.functions.ts:353-362`** — `promotePrdToTasks` batch-inserts tasks correctly, then loops `recordLineage()` once per task:
```ts
const edges = (tasks ?? []).map((t) => ({
  user_id: userId, parent_kind: "prd", parent_id: prd.id,
  child_kind: "task", child_id: t.id, relation: "promoted",
  rationale: "Generated from PRD by promotePrdToTasks", created_by_agent: "prd-writer",
}));
if (edges.length) {
  const { error } = await supabase.from("artifact_lineage")
    .upsert(edges, { onConflict: "user_id,parent_kind,parent_id,child_kind,child_id,relation" });
  if (error) console.error("promotePrdToTasks: batch lineage upsert failed:", error);
}
```

**1.4 — `src/lib/capabilities.functions.ts:250,255,256,270`** — inside `buildCapabilityForAgent`, four more queries fire per-agent (station skills, disabled skills, tool modes, graduation/capability history) that weren't caught by the 07-18 fix (which only hoisted the voice/brief/rules invariants). For 34 cast members that's ~170 round trips to render the Capabilities panel. Same hoist-and-`.in()` pattern already used for voice/brief/rules applies here.

---

**2.1 — `src/components/knowledge/CalendarPanel.tsx:316-360`** — `allItems`/`feed`/`pastCount`/`buckets` are plain `const`s (filter+sort+groupBy chains) recomputed on every render. The component has 9 `useMutation`s whose `isPending` flips, plus local form state (`editTitle`, etc.), trigger full re-renders on every keystroke:
```tsx
// before
const allItems = [...meetingItems, ...eventItems];
const feed = allItems.filter(/* ... */).sort(/* ... */);
const pastCount = allItems.filter(/* ... */).length;
const buckets: Record<string, DayItem[]> = {};
for (const it of allItems) { /* ... */ }

// after
const allItems = useMemo(() => [...meetingItems, ...eventItems], [meetingItems, eventItems]);
const { feed, pastCount, buckets } = useMemo(() => {
  const _now = Date.now();
  // ...same logic...
  return { feed, pastCount, buckets };
}, [allItems]);
```
⚠️ Caveat: the 14-day window boundary uses live `Date.now()` today. Memoizing on `[allItems]` pins that boundary to the last data change rather than true "now" — a small, probably-desirable behavior change (avoids flicker), but worth a conscious call, not a blind wrap.

**2.2 — `src/components/chat/ModelSwitcher.tsx:64`** — `modelsByProvider(MODELS)` regroups a static, module-level constant array on every render. `ModelSwitcher` lives in the chat composer, which re-renders at streaming-token frequency:
```ts
// hoist to module scope, outside the component — zero render-time dependencies
const MODEL_GROUPS = modelsByProvider(MODELS);
// inside the component: const groups = MODEL_GROUPS;
```

---

**3.1 — `src/lib/ai/runtime.server.ts:466-490` (`resolveCallKey`), invoked from `attempt()` at `:1624-1656`** — BYOK-eligibility + credit-account resolution (workspace lookup, `ensure_user_default_account` RPC, `plan_tier` select) re-runs identically on every retry (`maxRetries` default 2) *and* every fallback-chain model — up to 3-6x per `callModel` call, for input (`userId`/`workspaceId`) that never changes within that call. Worst exactly when the system is already under load (a 429/500 triggering retries):
```ts
// resolve once, before the retry loop
const cachedAccountId = await resolveCreditAccountId(supabase, userId, opts.workspaceId ?? null);
const cachedByokAllowed = opts.byoOverride ? true : await byokAllowedForCall(supabase, userId, opts.workspaceId, cachedAccountId);
const attempt = async (model: string) => {
  const keyInfo = await resolveCallKey(supabase, userId, prov, opts.byoOverride, opts.workspaceId, opts.surface,
    { accountId: cachedAccountId, byokAllowed: cachedByokAllowed });
  // ...
};
```

**3.2 — `src/lib/connectors/resolve.server.ts:220-418` (`resolveProviderAuth`)** — no in-process caching at all (up to 4-5 sequential lookups: tier, binding, connection, membership), unlike `runtime.server.ts`'s `creditsEnabled()`, which already has a proven 5-minute in-process TTL cache for exactly this kind of rarely-changing data. No confirmed repeat-call-site was found (each cron caller invokes it once per due workspace — inherent multi-tenant work, not literal N+1), but the `plan_tier` lookup is a strong candidate to reuse the existing TTL pattern, since this function is the credential chokepoint for every external connector call platform-wide.

---

Swept every `setInterval`/`EventSource`/`AbortController`/manual-subscription across the persistent chrome components and several polling panels — **all clean**: matched `clearInterval`s, React Query's auto-cleaned `refetchInterval`, and a correctly-unsubscribed `supabase.auth.onAuthStateChange` listener in `BudgetBar.tsx`.

**4.1 — Low — `src/components/supaprod/AmbientChip.tsx:108-179`** — the geolocation/weather resolution effect chains several async calls (`loadFromBrowserPosition`, `loadFromNetworkLocation`, `loadFromTimeZone`) with no cancellation flag. If the component unmounts mid-flight (e.g. the geolocation permission prompt is still pending), the resolved promise calls `setPlace`/`setWeather`/`setDenied` on an unmounted component:
```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  let cancelled = false;
  const applyPayload = (payload: AmbientPayload) => {
    if (cancelled) return;
    setPlace(payload.place); setWeather(payload.weather); setDenied(false);
  };
  // ...same chain, guard the final .catch(() => setDenied(true)) too...
  return () => { cancelled = true; };
}, []);
```
Practical risk is low — `AmbientChip` lives in the persistent top bar and rarely actually unmounts — but it's cheap to close.

